 ### Establish responsibilities before expanding a large plan
 
-When one coherent scope would demand too much simultaneous context, identify **territories**: bounded areas of design responsibility with owned decisions, state or rules, allocated obligations, external dependencies, and integration duties. Reuse existing scope or component identities where suitable. Keep small work in one scope when a split adds more coordination than it removes.
-
-Propose a small initial set once enough is known to choose useful boundaries, before expanding all local detail. Record material boundary choices with their actual authority and provisional conditions. Preserve fixed boundaries unless a supported issue warrants change; do not force a partition before the evidence supports it.
-
-Choose territories around coherent responsibility and change locality, not page counts or available agents. A territory is not automatically a repository, service, deployment unit, PRD, agent, or delivery phase. Do not change runtime architecture just to shorten a document. Where responsibilities must remain tightly coupled, divide the authoring or review questions and define how their conclusions join instead of forcing an artificial architectural split.
-
-Keep the shared system brief small: purpose, protected constraints, major responsibilities, shared decisions, canonical locations, material dependencies, and integrated outcomes. Place local detail with its owner and a shared rule with the smallest scope capable of owning it. The shared brief must not become a second copy of every territory's design. Link to one controlling definition for each fact, obligation, and contract.
